    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">44 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Julius Caesar is assassinated and his will names Octavius his main heir, with the condition that he take Caesar's name</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">43 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Octavian marches on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, becomes consul at 19 and forms the triumvirate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Lepidus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">42 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Antony and Octavian defeat Brutus and Cassius in two battles at Philippi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">31 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Agrippa defeats Antony and Cleopatra at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">30 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Antony and Cleopatra take their own lives and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> passes under Octavian's direct control</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">27 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Octavian returns power to the Senate, takes back most provinces and receives the title augustus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">23 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After a severe illness he resigns the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> and receives tribunician power for life</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14 CE</span><span class="rf-text">Augustus dies at Nola; he is succeeded by his stepson and adopted son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and deified by the Senate</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gaius Octavius was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 23 September 63 BCE, at a family property on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatine-hill">Palatine Hill</a>. His father, also Gaius Octavius, came from a moderately wealthy equestrian family of the gens Octavia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velletri">Velitrae</a>, near Rome, rose through the cursus honorum and governed Macedonia; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> the boy was given the cognomen Thurinus to commemorate his father's victory over followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a> at Thurii. His mother, Atia, was a niece of Julius Caesar. After his father died in 59 or 58 BCE, Atia married Lucius Marcius Philippus, consul in 56 BCE.</p><p>His first public appearance was the funeral oration for his grandmother Julia, Caesar's sister, in 52 or 51 BCE. A Greek slave tutor, Sphaerus, taught him reading, writing, arithmetic and Greek, and as a teenager he studied philosophy under Areios of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and Athenodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and rhetoric under Marcus Epidius and Apollodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>. He grew up during the civil war between Caesar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. In 47 BCE, after he took the toga virilis, Caesar had him elected pontiff; poor health kept him from the African campaign, but in 45 BCE he joined Caesar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, and on 13 September 45 BCE Caesar deposited a will naming him principal heir.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustus&#8221; cites where it covers Early life:</p><ul><li>Pat Southern, Augustus (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Karl Galinsky, Augustus: Introduction to the Life of an Emperor (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mark-antony.jpg" alt="Mark Antony" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octavian_and_Antony_denarius_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Byzantium565, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Octavius was at Apollonia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> when Caesar was assassinated on 15 March 44 BCE. The will left him three-quarters of Caesar's estate on condition that he take Caesar's name; against his stepfather's advice he accepted, called himself Gaius Julius Caesar, and is known to historians as Octavian. He took 700 million sesterces stored at Brundisium for Caesar's Parthian war, seized the tribute of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> without permission and, by June, had raised 3,000 veterans. He contested the leadership of the Caesarians with the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> attacked Antony in a series of speeches.</p><p>When Antony besieged Decimus Brutus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Mutina</a>, Cicero had Octavian made a senator on 1 January 43 BCE with a legal command. Both consuls died in the battles of Forum Gallorum and Mutina, leaving Octavian with their armies. Denied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and on 19 August 43 BCE, aged 19, became consul. In October he, Antony and Lepidus formed the triumvirate, legalised by the lex Titia. Their proscriptions outlawed some 300 senators and equestrians and confiscated thousands of properties, and in October 42 BCE they defeated Brutus and Cassius at Philippi. In the following years Octavian fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-antonius">Lucius Antonius</a> at Perusia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sextus-pompey">Sextus Pompey</a> at sea, removed Lepidus in 36 BCE, and broke with Antony, who was allied with Cleopatra. Agrippa defeated them at Actium on 2 September 31 BCE, and in 30 BCE both took their own lives.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustus&#8221; cites where it covers Heir to Caesar, Growing tensions, Second Triumvirate:</p><ul><li>Pat Southern, Augustus (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Werner Eck, The Age of Augustus (Blackwell Publishing, 2003)</li><li>Adrian Goldsworthy, Augustus: First Emperor of Rome (Yale University Press, 2014)</li><li>Henry Thompson Rowell, Rome in the Augustan Age (University of Oklahoma Press, 1962)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ara-pacis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ara-pacis.jpg" alt="Ara Pacis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ara-pacis">Ara Pacis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ara_Pacis_(SW).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rabax63, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 13 January 27 BCE Octavian publicly returned power to the Senate; three days later he accepted a ten-year charge over provinces that held most of the legions, and the Senate gave him the title augustus, "the revered". A second settlement in 23 BCE gave him greater proconsular power across the empire and the power of a tribune for life; he later added imperium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> itself, became pontifex maximus in 12 BCE and was named pater patriae in 2 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which he ruled through an equestrian prefect, was treated as his own property.</p><p>His armies conquered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>, Noricum, Illyricum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, annexed Galatia and extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> Proconsularis; Judea was added to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. In 20 BCE he recovered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> the standards lost by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a>, and a treaty with Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanirenas">Amanirenas</a> of Kush set the new border at Maharraqa. In Rome he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a> in 27 BCE and the vigiles, a fire brigade and police force, in 6 CE, fixed a standing army of 28 legions of about 170,000 soldiers with a military treasury, replaced the tax farmers with salaried collectors and assessed taxes by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. He said that he had found Rome a city of bricks and left it one of marble, raising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ara-pacis">Ara Pacis</a>, the Forum of Augustus and many temples, and was a patron of poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustus&#8221; cites where it covers Written works, Revenue reforms, Post-classical visual artworks:</p><ul><li>Werner Eck, The Age of Augustus (Blackwell Publishing, 2003)</li><li>Karl Galinsky, Augustus: Introduction to the Life of an Emperor (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li><li>Mary Beard, Twelve Caesars: Images of Power from the Ancient World to the Modern (Princeton University Press, 2021)</li><li>Adrian Goldsworthy, Augustus: First Emperor of Rome (Yale University Press, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/caesarion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caesarion.jpg" alt="Caesarion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesarion_granite_head.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">carolemage (Flickr), CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Octavian's rise was violent. Contemporary historians disagree which triumvir was most responsible for the proscriptions, but agree that all three used them to eliminate enemies. His veteran settlements evicted whole populations from some eighteen cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and after Perusia surrendered in February 40 BCE he had 300 senators and equestrians executed on the anniversary of Caesar's death, reprisals that sullied his reputation; Propertius disapproved of the executions of prisoners. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarion">Caesarion</a>, Cleopatra's son by Caesar, and Antony's son Antyllus killed.</p><p>Under the principate, the Marcus Primus affair of 23 BCE raised the charge that he had ordered a war in a senatorial province, which he denied under oath; some jurors seem to have disbelieved him. The conspirators led by Fannius Caepio were executed as soon as they were captured, without giving testimony, and he concealed these events. As triumvir he destroyed the public records from 44 to 36 BCE, and around 8 CE he exiled the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>. The jurist Marcus Antistius Labeo openly criticised his regime; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> wrote that he cunningly reduced Republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, while calling him benign and moderate, saw him as an autocrat. Ronald <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syme">Syme</a> in The Roman Revolution (1939) called him a "sickly and sinister youth" whose faction resembled a crime syndicate.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustus&#8221; cites where it covers First conflict with Antony, Ancient and contemporary views, Modern perspectives:</p><ul><li>Adrian Goldsworthy, Augustus: First Emperor of Rome (Yale University Press, 2014)</li><li>Pat Southern, Augustus (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Werner Eck, The Age of Augustus (Blackwell Publishing, 2003)</li><li>Kitty Chisholm, Rome: The Augustan Age; A Source Book (Oxford University Press, in association with the Open University Press, 1981)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tiberius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiberius.jpg" alt="Tiberius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Tiberius_Portrait_of_the_%27Imperium_Maius%27_type_in_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The succession troubled Augustus from his illness in 23 BCE. His nephew Marcellus, married to his daughter Julia, died that year; Julia then married Agrippa, whose sons Gaius and Lucius Augustus adopted as heirs. Agrippa died in 12 BCE and Augustus made his stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> divorce Vipsania to marry Julia; Tiberius's brother Drusus died in 9 BCE. Augustus exiled Julia for adultery in 2 BCE. After Lucius died in 2 CE and Gaius in 4 CE, he recalled Tiberius and adopted him in June 4 CE, on condition that Tiberius adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>. He exiled his youngest grandson, Agrippa Postumus, and disowned him.</p><p>His last years brought hard news from the frontiers: an Illyrian revolt from 6 to 9 CE, and in 9 CE the destruction of three legions under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>, after which he advised against further conquest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 8 CE he handed foreign embassies to three ex-consuls, and by 13 CE Tiberius held power equal to his. His health declined in the months before his death at Nola, where his father had died, on 19 August 14 CE. His reported last words asked whether he had played the part well. Tiberius and his son Drusus gave the eulogy, the body was cremated near his mausoleum, and the Senate deified him.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustus&#8221; cites where it covers The search for an heir, Tiberius, heir to Augustus, Death of Augustus, Deification:</p><ul><li>Pat Southern, Augustus (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Werner Eck, The Age of Augustus (Blackwell Publishing, 2003)</li><li>Adrian Goldsworthy, Augustus: First Emperor of Rome (Yale University Press, 2014)</li><li>Ronald Syme, The Augustan Aristocracy (Oxford University Press, 1986)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pax-romana.jpg" alt="Pax Romana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana">Pax Romana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Aurelianus,_Carin_et_Numérien,_Lyon_-_btv1b113442700_(2_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carin (empereur romain). Autorité émettrice de monnaie présumée Numérien (0254?-0284; empereur romain). Autorité émettrice de monnaie présu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Augustus created a regime that kept relative peace and prosperity for two centuries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana">Pax Romana</a> or Pax Augusta, though the myth of an Augustan golden age may obscure the difficulties he faced. The principate lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crisis-of-the-third-century">Crisis of the Third Century</a>, and his regime laid foundations for the idea of universal monarchy in the Byzantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empires</a>. The names Caesar and augustus became titles of Roman rulers for fourteen centuries, Caesar the root of kaiser and czar and imperator of "emperor". Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> wished each emperor after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> to be "more fortunate than Augustus and better than Trajan". His Res Gestae, copied throughout the empire, was called by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-mommsen">Theodor Mommsen</a> the "queen of inscriptions".</p><p>Church fathers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> presented him as a divinely ordained peacemaker for the coming of Christ, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> as a righteous ruler. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonathan-swift">Jonathan Swift</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> and Thomas Blackwell saw a usurper or tyrant; the 19th century more often a reformer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> celebrated his bimillenary in 1937-1938, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syme">Syme</a>'s work, written against that background, shaped scholarship in the English-speaking world. Werner Eck and Sarolta Takács credit him with a standing professional army, dynastic succession and long peace; Adrian Goldsworthy in 2014 called him essentially a military dictator, but no more ruthless than other warlords, and judged comparisons with modern dictators anachronistic.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustus&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy, Overview, Enduring institutions, Month of August:</p><ul><li>Adrian Goldsworthy, Augustus: First Emperor of Rome (Yale University Press, 2014)</li><li>Pat Southern, Augustus (Routledge, 2014)</li><li>Werner Eck, The Age of Augustus (Blackwell Publishing, 2003)</li><li>Karl Galinsky, Augustus: Introduction to the Life of an Emperor (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Augustus, born Gaius Octavius, rose as Julius Caesar's heir through civil war, proscriptions and the defeat of Antony and Cleopatra, and from 27 BCE ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> behind republican forms. His reign of conquest, reform and building began two centuries of relative peace; ancient and modern critics have called it an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>.</em></p>

